1. Difference Between a Class and an Interface
-A Class is like a blueprint of an object. It describes what an object
is (its state and its actions). A class can have variables and methods with full code.
-An Interface is like a contract. It describes what an object can do.
It only lists the names of the methods, but it doesn't say how to do them.
The class that "signs" the contract must write the code for those methods.

2. Fields in Interfaces
Can they have fields? Yes, every field is automatically "public static final"

3. Multiple Inheritance of Interfaces
A class can only have one parent clas, but a class can implement many interfaces. "implements"
An interface can have many interfaces for this "extends".

